*This project has been created as part of the 42 curriculum by agard.*

# Philosophers — *I never thought philosophy would be so deadly*

## Description

This project is an introduction to concurrent programming using threads and mutexes in C.

The simulation places N philosophers around a round table. Each philosopher alternates between three states: **eating**, **sleeping**, and **thinking**. Between each pair of philosophers lies a single fork. To eat, a philosopher must hold both the fork to their left and the fork to their right. If a philosopher goes too long without eating, they die — and the simulation stops.

The challenge is to manage concurrent access to shared resources (the forks) without deadlocks, data races, or starvation.

## Instructions

### Compilation

```bash
make
```

### Execution

```bash
./philo <number_of_philosophers> <time_to_die> <time_to_eat> <time_to_sleep> [number_of_times_each_philosopher_must_eat]
```

| Argument | Description |
|---|---|
| `number_of_philosophers` | Number of philosophers (and forks) at the table |
| `time_to_die` (ms) | Time before a philosopher dies if they haven't started eating |
| `time_to_eat` (ms) | Time it takes to eat (requires two forks) |
| `time_to_sleep` (ms) | Time spent sleeping |
| `number_of_times_each_philosopher_must_eat` | *(optional)* Simulation stops when all philosophers have eaten this many times |

### Examples

```bash
# 5 philosophers, should not die
./philo 5 800 200 200

# 5 philosophers, each must eat 7 times
./philo 5 800 200 200 7

# 1 philosopher, must die
./philo 1 800 200 200
```

### Test with valgrind / helgrind

```bash
valgrind --tool=helgrind ./philo 5 800 200 200
```

